Patch 7909 (ENHANCEMENT): PlayStation pads (DualSense, DualSense Edge, DualShock 4 v1 and v2) over USB or Bluetooth, up to four, each its own device, alongside XInput pads.

Extends 6503 (one DualSense, port 0 only, when XInput had nothing there) and 7908 (the join table). Windows has no XInput driver for these pads, so
with INPUT_MODE=controller a tester's PS4 or PS5 pad was not seen at all unless it was a single DualSense on port 0.

Pads: vendor 054C, product 0CE6 / 0DF2 (DualSense, Edge) and 05C4 / 09CC (DualShock 4 v1, v2). A manager thread looks at the HID devices every second
while fewer than four pads are read (never on the game thread; the path is checked for the vendor id before a device is opened, and a pad must be
a Generic Desktop Joystick / Game Pad collection) and starts one reader thread per pad, which keeps the newest input report of that pad (the read buffer
is as long as the device's InputReportByteLength). A pad that is unplugged makes its read fail, the reader ends and frees its slot, and a later pad takes
the lowest free slot. A Bluetooth pad is told apart by InputReportByteLength > 64 and switched from its short report to the full one by reading one
feature report (DualSense 0x05 as in 6503, DualShock 4 0x02).

Report layouts (pad_hid.h; pure code, tested by installer/tools/pad_join_test.c): from the PS4 developer wiki pages "DualShock 4" (USB and Bluetooth
reports) and "DualSense", the byte offsets are written out in the header; no code of another project was copied. DualShock 4: USB report 0x01 (64
bytes) with LX LY RX RY at bytes 1..4, hat and face buttons at 5, shoulders / Share / Options / stick clicks at 6, L2 R2 analog at 8 and 9; Bluetooth
report 0x11 (the same data two bytes later); short Bluetooth report 0x01 (10 bytes) before the switch. DualSense as 6503 read it. The pad is turned into
the same state an XInput pad gives (Cross = A, Circle = B, Square = X, Triangle = Y, Share or Create = BACK, Options = START), so the PAD_BTN_* rebinds,
the deadzone and the trigger threshold apply to it unchanged (pad_xinput_to_host is the old XInput mapping, now a function).

Which port: the join table (7908) has devices 5..8 for the HID pads (PJ_DEV_HID0 + n; the keyboard stays 4), so a reorder (the port's menu row or the
chord Select + L3 + R3, the Share or Create button is Select) treats every PlayStation pad like any other: first press, lowest free player, up to four.
Without a reorder, the old order stays and HID pads come after it: XInput pads keep their slot as their port, then the HID pads take the lowest ports
no XInput pad holds, in the order the readers have them (the order Windows listed them at plug-in; a pad that leaves frees its reader slot). Chosen
because it changes nothing that works today: a lone Xbox pad is its slot, a lone DualSense is port 0 (it was the stand-in for slot 0, now it is the
first HID pad and the lowest free port is 0), and a DualSense next to an Xbox pad on slot 1 is still port 0. The cases that change were
unusable before: a DualSense next to an Xbox pad on slot 0 (it was ignored) is now port 1, and a DualShock 4 is a pad at all. For a fixed order the
reorder is the tool; this patch adds no setting for the default order.

The 6503 stand-in for slot 0 is gone (the DualSense is a device of its own now). A pad on port 0 in the old layout is an XInput pad or, failing that,
the first HID pad, so the keyboard rules of 7908 (a keyboard fills port 0 only when no pad is there) are as before.

PAD_NO_PLAYSTATION=1 turns all HID pads off; PAD_NO_DUALSENSE=1 stays as a second name for the same switch (it now also covers the DualShock 4).
No rumble, no lightbar, no touchpad or motion: ASSUMPTION: rumble over raw HID needs the pad's output report with its own checksum over Bluetooth,
which is not trivial or safe to get right without a pad to test; the game gets a pad without rumble (as 6503 had it). Rumble keeps going to XInput
devices only (7908's device test, devices below 4).

Not tested with real pads (none here): the parsing is tested on reports built from the documented layout (host test, address and undefined-behaviour
sanitizers), the Windows code only compiled (MinGW -fsyntax-only -Wall -Wextra). The Bluetooth DualShock 4 read size and its switch feature report
(0x02, 37 bytes) are from the same documentation and unproven.

